  "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B+\\pi I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B+\\pi I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $B$ must be a $22\\times 22$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $10,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{3}=22-10=12.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B+\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ of dimension $12$ in ${R}^{22}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{4}=22-8=14.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B+\\pi I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ of dimension $14$ in ${R}^{22}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $12$ in $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $14,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{4}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(B+\\pi I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(B+\\pi I)^{3}=14-12=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\pi .$",
